IR::Block's instruction pool starts with one slab of 4096 instructions (sizeof(Inst) * 4096, about 200 KB), malloc'd and
freed for every block translated. On a host where malloc of that size is a cheap mmap that costs nothing; on Symbian it is
above RHybridHeap's paged-allocation threshold, so each translation commits and zero-fills ~50 pages (Exec::ChunkAdjust)
and frees them again -- a Qt application (tens of thousands of blocks) spends minutes there before it draws a frame. The
pool grows by whole slabs when a block needs more, so 64-instruction slabs (a few KB, the heap's ordinary cells) keep
every block's capacity and leave the kernel out of translation.
--- a/src/dynarmic/ir/basic_block.cpp
+++ b/src/dynarmic/ir/basic_block.cpp
@@ -22,7 +22,7 @@
 namespace Dynarmic::IR {

 Block::Block(const LocationDescriptor& location)
-        : location{location}, end_location{location}, cond{Cond::AL}, instruction_alloc_pool{std::make_unique<Common::Pool>(sizeof(Inst), 4096)} {}
+        : location{location}, end_location{location}, cond{Cond::AL}, instruction_alloc_pool{std::make_unique<Common::Pool>(sizeof(Inst), 64)} {}

 Block::~Block() = default;

